{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And he came as far as Hebron.</b> Caleb alone went there and prostrated himself on the graves of the fathers, so that he would not be drawn into the plot of his companions. And so it says (Deuteronomy 1): ״וְלוֹ אֶתֵּן אֶת הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר דָּרַךְ בָּהּ״, and it is written (Judges 1): ״וַיִּתְּנוּ לְכָלֵב אֶת חֶבְרוֹן״ (Sotah 34)." "<b>Seven years was it built.</b> Is it possible that Ham built Hebron for Canaan his youngest son before he built Zoan for Mitzrayim his eldest son? Rather, it was fully built, and better than one of the seven of Zoan. And he comes to inform you of the praise of the land of Israel: there are no rockier ground in the land of Israel than Hebron — therefore He set it aside for graves of the dead — and you have no land more excellent in all the lands than Egypt, as it says ״כְּגַן יְהוָה כְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ (Genesis 13), and Zoan is the finest part of the land of Egypt, for there is the seat of the kings, as it says ״כִּי הָיוּ בְצֹעַן שָׂרָיו״ (Isaiah 30), and Hebron was better than it sevenfold (Sotah 34; Ketubot 112)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:10:46.050361347Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}